      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion in the same loop or domain",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Endogenous receptor tracked at the cell surface and through endosomal compartments; surface display confirmed by SBP-streptavidin pulldown and EGFP fluorescence imaging. Receptor trafficking function preserved (used as model for endosomal sorting studies).",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "TfR1 is a type II single-pass transmembrane protein with its N-terminus intracellular (residues 1-65) and its entire ectodomain at the C-terminal side (residues 87-760). The paper names no specific residue for the fusion, but TfR1 has exactly ONE extracellular terminus \u2014 the C-terminus at residue 760 (F). For SBP to be accessible to streptavidin on the cell surface, it must be extracellular; the only extracellular terminus is the C-terminus. Therefore insert_after_residue=760 is topology-derived, not chosen. The CRISPR knock-in at the endogenous locus confirms the tagged receptor is expressed at physiological levels and retains trafficking function. Residue 760 (F) confirmed from the numbered sequence; residue_after is empty (C-terminal append). Distance from TM boundary (residue 86) to C-terminus is 674 residues, well clear of the >=3 residue constraint. Endogenous TfR in SUM159 breast cancer cells was CRISPR-Cas9 knock-in tagged with SBP and EGFP at the C-terminus for surface receptor tracking and streptavidin-based pulldown assays. The tagged receptor was used to study endosomal trafficking and recycling of the surface-displayed receptor. [validation: surface_and_function; position: topology_derived; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 33523982,
          "citation": "PMID 33523982",
          "claim": "The CRISPR-Cas9 strategy that we previously described ( 22 ) was used to engineer the breast cancer\u2013derived SUM159 cells to express endogenous TfR fused to the streptavidin-binding peptide (SBP) and enhanced green fluorescent protein (EGFP)."
        },
        {
          "citation": "preprint",
          "claim": "The CRISPR/Cas9 strategy that we previously described (21) was used to engineer the breast cancer-derived SUM159 cells to express endogenous TfR fused to the streptavidin-binding peptide (SBP) and EGFP."
        },
        {
          "pmid": 42816619,
          "citation": "PMID 42816619",
          "claim": "All receptors contain an N-terminal FLAG tag, with an additional N-terminal HA tag or C-terminal GFP fusion in some cases, and were co-expressed with TfR1-v5 in HEK293T or Expi293 cells."
        }
      ],
      "plddt": null,
